# A coding agent

A coding agent is the loop you've watched Claude Code or Pi run: read the repo, make one
change, run the tests, read the output, decide what's next — until it's green. Most of
that is *plumbing*: a tool protocol, a sandbox to run commands in, a transcript, a
budget, a way to add new abilities. Workbooks ships all of it as primitives, so the part
you write is small — and this lesson shows where each piece lives: the brain, where it
runs, how it's contained, how you give it new powers, and how you run a fleet.

## The brain

An [`agent`](/tooling/authoring-an-agent) block is a unit whose **body is its system
prompt**. The runtime gives it exactly one tool — `bash` — and the agent does everything
by running commands in it:

```
agent :coder do
  You are a coding agent working in /work — a checkout of the repository. Your one tool
  is bash. Run `kits` to see what's available and `help <kit>` for a kit's commands.

  Work like a careful engineer:
    1. Look first — read the relevant files and run the tests to see the current state.
    2. Change one thing — the smallest edit that moves toward the goal.
    3. Verify — re-run the tests and READ the output. Never assume a change worked.
    4. Repeat, or stop — when the tests pass and the goal is met, summarize what you
       changed and why.

  Never guess a result you could get by running a command. Keep edits minimal and
  reversible. Stuck twice on the same error? Step back and re-read the code.
end
```

There is no tool registry to wire and no agent framework — the prose *is* the program.

## Where it runs, and how it's contained

This is the part that matters for trusting an agent with your repo:

- **Placement — it runs on the server.** `agent` is a server-side kind: the loop runs on
  the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus) (the BEAM), not in the browser. You don't
  choose client/server per call — the *kind* decides. A `client` block would be the UI;
  the agent is the brain behind it. → [Placement & languages](/language/placement-and-languages)
- **Sandbox — `bash` is not your shell.** Every command the agent runs is a **wasm kit**
  executed in wasmtime against a `/work` virtual filesystem. The agent can read and write
  files *in `/work`* and run those wasm commands — and nothing else. It cannot reach the
  host filesystem, spawn host processes, or open a raw socket. The sandbox is the wall;
  the agent is powerful *inside* it. → [Sandboxing & the Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock)
- **The web is brokered.** Reading or operating the web (`fetch`, `scrape`, `navigate`)
  goes through a host capability that is SSRF-gated — the guest never gets raw network.

So "let an autonomous agent edit and run code" is safe by construction: the blast radius
is one `/work` sandbox.

## Its capabilities are kits — and you can add one

An agent's abilities are its **kits**: `coreutils` (the unix base — `ls`, `cat`, `sed`,
`grep`…) and `web` ship by default. To give the agent a *new* power, you don't edit the
agent — you author a [`toolkit`](/tooling/toolkits), a small CLI compiled to a wasm
command that instantly shows up in `bash`:

```
toolkit :rg do
  // summary: ripgrep-style search — rg <pattern> over /work
  #include <stdio.h>
  // …a real grep/ripgrep in C, compiled to wasm32-wasi…
  int main(int argc, char **argv) { /* search stdin/files for argv[1] */ }
end
```

Now `rg` is a command the `:coder` can run — capability granted, sandboxed like every
other kit. That's the model: **capabilities are composable CLIs**, not bespoke tool
definitions. (The `sandbox` kind takes the complementary path — guest code with explicit
`grant: [net|kv|fs|…]` capabilities; see [Grants & capabilities](/capabilities/grants-and-capabilities).)

## The loop you don't have to write

When the agent runs, the runtime drives the loop for you: send the instructions + task to
the model advertising `bash` → if it calls `bash`, run the command in the `/work` sandbox
and feed the output back → repeat (think, run, read) → stop when the model answers without
a tool call, or the wall-clock budget expires. You never touch the tool-call wiring,
transcript, or context-window management.

## Giving it a task

An agent's body is just its `system` prompt, so you run it from a [`server`](/language/placement-and-languages)
with `Nexus.Agent.run/1`: it seeds files into `/work`, runs the loop, and returns the
final answer plus whatever the agent wrote. Behind a [route](/serving/routes-and-pages):

```
server :fix do
  # @coder is the same instruction text as the :coder block above
  @coder "You are a coding agent working in /work. Your one tool is bash … (as above)"

  route "POST /fix", :run

  def run(req) do
    case Nexus.Agent.run(
           system: @coder,
           task: req.body["task"],        # "make the failing date test pass"
           seed: req.body["files"],       # %{"lib/date.ex" => "…", "test/date_test.exs" => "…"}
           timeout_ms: 180_000
         ) do
      {:ok, %{answer: summary, vfs_files: files}} -> %{summary: summary, files: files}
      {:error, reason} -> {500, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
    end
  end
end
```

`seed` writes the repo into the sandbox; `vfs_files` hands back the edited files — the
diff. The agent did the reading, editing, and test-running in between.

## Running a fleet

Because a run is a function call, doing a hundred is a `map`. Fan a coder across every
failing ticket; the BEAM runs thousands of lightweight processes, so they go at once:

```
server :sweep do
  def run(tickets) do
    tickets
    |> Enum.map(fn t ->
      Task.async(fn -> Nexus.Agent.run(system: @coder, task: t.body, seed: t.repo) end)
    end)
    |> Task.await_many(:infinity)
  end
end
```

One agent or a thousand is the same code — only the list gets longer. That fan-out is the
[agents-at-scale](/examples/agents-at-scale) pattern; swap the worker for any job.

## What to take away

- **The agent is prose**; the loop, tools, and sandbox are the runtime's job.
- **It runs server-side, sandboxed** — `bash` = wasm kits over a `/work` VFS, web brokered.
- **Capabilities are kits** — add one by authoring a [`toolkit`](/tooling/toolkits), no
  agent change.
- **Running it is `Nexus.Agent.run/1`**, so scaling it is a `Task.async` map.
